  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday: A dry weather pattern will dominate\nthrough Friday as an upper ridge builds over the region. The\nonly chance for precipitation will come this morning mainly along\nthe Cascade crest as a weakening mid level wave tracks across \nthe region. The main influence from this will be passing mid and\nhigh level clouds. The building ridge will allow for a gradual\nwarming trend with high temperatures peaking on Friday in the\nmid 60s to mid 70s.\n\nSaturday and Sunday: The ridge flattens on Saturday as a quick\nmoving cold front sweeps across the region. A combination of\nswift westerly flow, and the bulk of the moisture with this\nfront in the low to mid levels of the atmosphere, will keep\nprecipitation focused near the Cascade crest with dry weather\nfor Central and Eastern Washington into the Idaho Panhandle. The\ncold front will result in breezy to locally windy conditions\nacross the region especially the typical spots such as the East\nSlopes of the Cascades, Columbia Basin, West Plains, and\nPalouse. The NBM is carrying a 50-70 percent chance of wind\ngusts exceeding 30 MPH in these areas, with a 40-50 percent\nchance of gusts exceeding 40 MPH on the Waterville Plateau.\nGiven the dry soils in the Columbia Basin can not rule out\npatchy blowing dust with the winds on Saturday. On Sunday as\nthis front exits dry weather and light winds return.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: The next system approaches the region but\nthe bulk of the ensembles (75 percent) show energy diving to the\nsouth into California. The remaining 25 percent show a weaker\ntrough passage over the region for a chance of showers. JW",
